data: {"type":"data-source","data":{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/recipes/format-dates.mdx","name":"format-dates.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}}

data: {"type":"tool-output-available","toolCallId":"call-f3020dc7-ccfc-4ef9-ab23-b10794088220","output":{"output":"<repo>github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs</repo>\n<path>content/en-US/docs/recipes/format-dates.mdx</path>\n<url>undefined</url>\n<content>\n1: # Formatting dates\n2: \n3: OpenMRS provides several utilities to work with dates. These utilities have been designed with locale sensitivity in mind. Acceptable date formats vary by language and region, and the functions are intended to accommodate that variation.\n4: \n5: ## Utilities for formatting dates for display\n6: \n7: ### formatDate\n8: \n9: This utility accepts a `Date` object and returns a string. It formats the input date according to the current locale and the given parameters:\n10: \n11: ```ts\n12: formatDate(date: Date, options?: {\n13:   mode?: \"standard\" | \"wide\",\n14:   time?: boolean | \"for today\",\n15:   day?: boolean,\n16:   month?: boolean,\n17:   year?: boolean,\n18:   noToday?: boolean,\n19:   locale?: string,\n20:   calendar?: string,\n21:   numberingSystem?: string\n22: }): string\n23: ```\n24: \n25: The mode parameter can be set to `standard` or `wide`.\n26: For English dates, `standard` mode displays a date such as `16-May-2023`, while `wide` mode displays a date such as `16 — May — 2023`. Other locales may use locale-specific month names, calendars, and punctuation.\n27: \n28: By default, `formatDate` uses `{ mode: \"standard\", time: \"for today\", day: true, month: true, year: true, noToday: false }`. If the date is today, then `Today` is produced instead of the actual day in the locale language, and the time is appended because the default `time` option is `\"for today\"`. Pass `{ time: false }` to suppress the time for today's date, or `{ noToday: true }` to format today like any other date.\n29: \n30: When the `time` option is set to `true`, the time is appended with a comma and a space, e.g. `16-May-2023, 08:21`. This format agrees with the output of `Date.prototype.toLocaleString` for most locales.\n31: \n32: Here's an example of how to use `formatDate` with a `Date` object:\n33: \n34: ```tsx\n35: import { formatDate } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n36: \n37: function AppointmentDetails({ appointment }: AppointmentDetailsProps) {\n38:   return <span>{formatDate(appointment.scheduledDate, { mode: \"standard\" })}</span>;\n39: }\n40: ```\n41: \n42: For an English locale, the returned value would be a string in the format `18-May-2023`.\n43: \n44: ### formatPartialDate\n45: \n46: Use `formatPartialDate` when a value may contain only a year or a year and month. This is common for approximate clinical dates. It parses the string using the current locale, hides date parts that were not present in the input, and then delegates formatting to `formatDate`.\n47: \n48: ```ts\n49: formatPartialDate(dateString: string, options?: Partial<FormatDateOptions>): string | null\n50: ```\n51: \n52: ```ts\n53: formatPartialDate(\"2021\"); // \"2021\"\n54: formatPartialDate(\"2021-04\"); // \"Apr 2021\"\n55: formatPartialDate(\"2021-04-09\"); // \"09-Apr-2021\"\n56: ```\n57: \n58: ### formatDatetime\n59: \n60: This utility function accepts a `Date` object and returns a `string`.\n61: It formats the input date to display both the date and time, separated by a comma, according to the current locale and the given options.\n62: \n63: `formatDatetime` accepts the same options as `formatDate` except for `time`, which is always included:\n64: \n65: ```ts\n66: formatDatetime(date: Date, options?: Partial<Omit<FormatDateOptions, \"time\">>): string\n67: ```\n68: \n69: Here's an example of how to use `formatDatetime`:\n70: \n71: ```tsx\n72: import { formatDatetime } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n73: \n74: function AppointmentDetails({ appointment }: AppointmentDetailsProps) {\n75:   return <span>{formatDatetime(appointment.scheduledDate)}</span>;\n76: }\n77: ```\n78: \n79: For an English locale, the returned value would be a string such as `18-May-2023, 08:21`.\n80: \n81: ### formatTime\n82: \n83: This utility function accepts a `Date` object and returns a string. It formats the time according to the current locale using the 12- or 24-hour format.\n84: \n85: ```ts\n86: formatTime(date: Date): string\n87: ```\n88: \n89: Here's an example of how to use `formatTime`:\n90: \n91: ```tsx\n92: import { formatTime } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n93: \n94: function AppointmentDetails({ appointment }: AppointmentDetailsProps) {\n95:   return <span>{formatTime(appointment.scheduledDate)}</span>;\n96: }\n97: ```\n98: \n99: The returned value depends on the locale. For example, it may be `08:21` in a 24-hour locale or `08:21 AM` in a 12-hour locale.\n100: \n101: ### Durations\n102: \n103: Use `formatDuration` when you already have a structured `Intl.DurationInput` value, or `formatDurationBetween` when you want to calculate and format the duration between two dates.\n104: \n105: ```ts\n106: formatDuration(duration: Intl.DurationInput, options?: Intl.DurationFormatOptions): string\n107: formatDurationBetween(\n108:   startDate: dayjs.ConfigType,\n109:   endDate?: dayjs.ConfigType,\n110:   options?: DurationUnit | DurationOptionsWithFormat\n111: ): string | null\n112: ```\n113: \n114: ```ts\n115: import { formatDurationBetween } from \"@openmrs/esm-framework\";\n116: \n117: formatDurationBetween(\"2022-01-01\", \"2024-07-30\"); // \"2 yrs\"\n118: formatDurationBetween(\"2022-01-01\", \"2024-07-30\", {\n119:   largestUnit: \"years\",\n120:   smallestUnit: \"days\",\n\n(Showing lines 1-120 of 193. Use offset=121 to continue.)\n</content>","sources":[{"type":"file","repo":"github.com/openmrs/openmrs-contrib-o3-docs","path":"content/en-US/docs/recipes/format-dates.mdx","name":"format-dates.mdx","revision":"HEAD"}]}}

